WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.600
An amateur map scout hunting Quebec campsites instead found a hidden impact crater from space. This is Fascinating Frontiers, episode 194. Nine nearby galaxies were examined in a recent study, actively growing supermassive black holes were found aligned with rings of new stars and shockwaves. Multi-wave length observations mapped gas flows and young stellar populations across the sample. Shockwaves from the black hole activity appear to compress gas

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:43.600
and trigger collapse. The data indicate these black holes drive processes that promote rather than suppress starbirth. Next up is an accidental find made right here on Earth. The searcher was looking for campsites in Quebec.

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:54.340
Satellite imagery revealed the crater feature during routine online map browsing. The discovery adds to the record of terrestrial impact sites identified remotely in Canada.

00:00:54.340 --> 00:01:07.380
The feature was noticed rather than found through a dedicated survey. No immediate fieldwork plans have been announced by researchers. Public imagery continues to reveal such structures without new ground expeditions.

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:16.420
The accidental discovery of an impact crater while scouting campsites shows how routine satellite imagery can still reveal new terrestrial features.

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:33.480
The find occurred in Quebec when an amateur astronomer examined online maps for potential camping locations rather than conducting a dedicated survey. Such remote identification adds to the global catalog of confirmed impact sites without requiring new dedicated observations.

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:40.720
The crater joins a growing list of structures first noticed through existing public imagery rather than ground expeditions.

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:52.600
Future searches may combine automated image analysis with citizen review to locate additional hidden craters. What other everyday map uses might unexpectedly expose planetary history.

00:01:53.220 --> 00:02:09.700
A compact particle detector on a cube sat multiplies solar storm warning time by a factor of 10. The instrument monitors solar energetic particles that can disrupt satellites and power grids. The sun's flares and storms remain the primary threat addressed by the monitoring.

00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:28.560
Early data indicate the extended warning interval could give operators more time to safeguard infrastructure. The cube sat carries the detector for continuous observation of space weather events. Now shifting to planets with extremely short orbits, newly reported worlds complete full orbits in three days.

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:40.200
The short periods place the planets in extremely close proximity to their stars. Such ultra short orbits are rare and offer opportunities to study atmospheric escape and tidal effects.

00:02:40.940 --> 00:02:53.240
Further observations are expected once the host systems are confirmed. No host star or distance details were supplied in the announcement. On a different note, a finished company has secured major new funding.

00:02:53.240 --> 00:03:04.180
ISI raised more than $1 billion in its series F round. The company is now addressing sovereign demand for synthetic aperture radar capabilities in multiple countries.

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:15.440
Expansion has proceeded rapidly since the funding round closed. ISI has opened new offices and secured contracts focused on national security and disaster monitoring applications.

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:29.660
The firm continues to grow its constellation of small radar satellites. Next, a recent launch plays two Earth observation satellites into orbit. The VV-30 flight lifted off from Europe's spaceport in French Guiana.

00:03:29.660 --> 00:03:52.680
Avio managed the launch without Ariana Space involvement. Lift off occurred at 10.21 pm local time. The rocket placed the two satellites into the plan's sun synchronous orbit. This marked the second time Avio conducted a Vega C mission independently. Both satellites acquired signals and are reported healthy in orbit. Commissioning activities have started for the pair.

00:03:52.680 --> 00:04:05.740
Flex will measure plant fluorescence to assess photosynthetic activity. Sentinel-3C tracks sea surface temperature and ocean color. Data from the two spacecraft will be cross-calibrated during the commissioning phase.

00:04:05.740 --> 00:04:25.040
Turkey has completed its first homegrown lunar spacecraft. The mission target remains 2027. Engineers completed integration and testing ahead of the planned launch window. The spacecraft represents Turkey's initial independent effort to reach the lunar surface. No further technical details were released.

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:48.840
A new test stand now supports engine development at the Houston spaceport. The facility will allow the company to conduct static fire tests of reusable rocket engines. Venus Aerospace focuses on hypersonic and space transportation technologies. No specific engine or timeline was announced for the first tests. The stand adds to the growing infrastructure at the Texas spaceport.

00:04:48.840 --> 00:05:03.500
The July 7 mission carried 79 payloads in total. 77 objects have now been catalogued from that launch. One payload remains aboard the ion tug. 11 tracked objects stay unidentified so far.

00:05:03.500 --> 00:05:18.480
Catalogging continues as additional tracking data arrives from ground stations. All three spear won satellites from USNRL, USSF underscore SSC, and near space launch remain among the unidentified objects.

00:05:19.180 --> 00:05:32.700
Better coordination with the 18th space defense squadron was expected. Failure of the satellites remains one possible explanation. The satellites were part of a technology demonstration effort involving multiple government and industry partners.

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:47.680
No signals have been confirmed from the trio to date. Picture a single carbon atom drifting through a molecular cloud the size of a large city, yet colder than any freezer on Earth, where temperatures sit only a few degrees above absolute zero.

00:05:47.680 --> 00:06:00.680
In that environment, atoms move so slowly that collisions should be too gentle to start new reactions. Laboratory work now shows carbon molecules can still link into longer chains through ion neutral reactions.

00:06:01.420 --> 00:06:12.680
One measured pathway proceeds at roughly one collision in every few thousand encounters. That rate remains fast enough on cosmic time scales to build detectable abundances of complex organics.

00:06:13.940 --> 00:06:23.880
Telescopes already see these molecules in the coldest cores where new stars will form. The exact branching ratios that favor certain chains over others remain uncertain.

